Focus cues, fusion, and stereopsis For a stimulus to be sharply focused on the retina, the eye must be accommodated to a distance close to the focal distance of the object. The acceptable range is the depth of focus, which is roughly ±0.3 diopters (D) under normal circumstances (Campbell, 1957; Charman & Whitefoot, 1977). Understandably, the blur caused by an accommodative error reduces the precision of stereopsis: With natural pupils, errors of 1D and 2D cause nearly two- and ten-

